C COLLECTIONS LIBRARY - MAN PAGE TREE
======================================

This directory holds real troff and groff manual pages for the library. There
is one file for each documented symbol, except for a family of related
constants, which shares one page named after the family.


What gets a page
----------------

A symbol gets a page only when somebody needs it to USE the library. That is
the whole test. A declaration in an installed header does not qualify a
symbol. Neither does a ccol_ prefix, nor being exported from the shared
object, nor being a static inline that a consumer could call.

Before you add a page, name the user-facing task that nobody can do without
that symbol. When there is no such task the symbol is internal, and it gets
no page, no section in README.md or in a guide under doc/, and no entry in
a SEE ALSO. You can name it in the prose of another page, to tell what
the symbol of that page does.

A name that starts with an underscore is internal, and the underscore is
there to say so at every call site. Such a name never gets a page. An
internal symbol can still live in an installed header and still be exported,
because a header cannot hide what the library itself needs across
translation units. The underscore, not the header and not the symbol table,
is what marks the boundary.

These are internal by that test, and none of them has a page:

    _csort_default_<type>_comparison_proc
        Every caller reaches these through csort_get_default_comparison_proc,
        which selects one from the type of its argument.

    _CCOL_CALCULATE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER_SIZE
        The size arithmetic that CCOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_RMEMPOOL_BUFFER
        applies for the caller.

    _ccol_find_nearest_gte_power_of_two, ccol_strdup, ccol_growbuf_*
        Helpers that the containers use for their own growth and storage.

A literal constant that matters only as an argument, a buffer size or a
field value of some function never gets a page of its own, even when a
caller types its name. Document it, with the value that it expands to, on
the page of each function or macro that takes it. A buffer length such as
CHTTPSVR_PEER_CERT_SHA256_LEN belongs on the page of the function that
fills the buffer, and a sentinel such as CHTTPSVR_NO_DEADLINE belongs where
the field that accepts it is described (chttpsvr_start(3)). A set of
constants that many unrelated functions share, such as the HTTP status
codes of CHTTP_STATUS(3), keeps one page of its own, so that the table is
written once.

Layout
------

    man/
      <module>/<symbol>.3   one page per raw C function or type-inferred macro
      <module>/<module>.7   module overview page, where present. It holds
                            the rules of the whole module that fit on no
                            single page
      common/ccollections.7 the overview of the whole library. It holds
                            the conventions that all modules share. Its
                            name is the name of the library and not
                            common.h. It therefore installs under a name
                            of its own.

<module> is the name of the header without the .h at the end. For example:
cvector, chashmap, cjson. The raw functions and their companion type-inferred
macros are together in the same module directory. Their names never collide,
so the file system needs no rule to tell them apart. The ".TH" line of each
page still records whether that page documents a function or a macro. The
fourth field reads "C Collections Library - Functions" or "C Collections
Library - Macros". The SYNOPSIS of each page shows the real declaration in
both cases. The SYNOPSIS and the EXAMPLES name each header the way an
application includes it from an install, as <ccollections/NAME.h>.

One symbol can have more than one name. An example is cjson_parse_mp and its
cjson_parse wrapper. Such a symbol gets one page with the content, and one or
more small ".so" redirect pages. This is how the glibc and BSD man pages
handle an alias.

A redirect page holds exactly one line, ".so <module>/<symbol>.3", and
nothing else: no ".TH" line and no comment. man then renders the target page
with the header and the footer of that page, and mandb records the alias as
a link to the target. The NAME section of the target lists every alias name,
so that whatis and apropos find each one.

A family of related constants is different. The CHTTP_STATUS_* constants of
chttp.h are one such family: they carry no behavior of their own, and a
reader wants the whole table at once. They share one page, man/chttp/
CHTTP_STATUS.3, whose NAME section lists every constant so that apropos and
whatis find each one. There are no redirect pages for the individual names,
so "man CHTTP_STATUS" opens the table and "man CHTTP_STATUS_OK" does not
resolve.

Viewing a page
--------------

`make install` puts every page from here into a real man3 and man7 tree. See
the root Makefile. After that, a plain `man cvector_create` works from any
directory, and so does every alias. A constant that shares a family page is
the exception; reach it by the name of that page.

You can also read a page without an install. Run man from inside this
directory, so that the ".so <module>/<symbol>.3" redirect of an alias page
resolves correctly. See above for those pages. "man -l" resolves a ".so"
target relative to the current working directory. It does not resolve it
relative to the location of the page:

    cd man && man -l cvector/cvector_create.3
    cd man && man -l cjson/cjson_parse.3   # an alias page, .so and all

Regenerating
------------

A person writes each of these pages. No tool generates them from the source
comments. When the documented behavior of a function or a macro changes, edit
the .3 file for that symbol directly. Keep the section order of the pages
around it: NAME, LIBRARY, SYNOPSIS, DESCRIPTION, PARAMETERS, RETURN VALUE,
NOTES, WARNINGS, THREAD SAFETY, EXAMPLES, SEE ALSO. Write every new page in
ASCII only.
